# This repository's contract is BYTES, so git must not rewrite a single one.
#
# Several checks compare files byte for byte, and several more embed a file's SHA-256 or its byte
# length in another file: the distribution notices, the source offer, the design-system pin, the
# generated token QML, the app icons, and the vendored upstream tree. On Windows, git checks out
# with core.autocrlf, every LF becomes CRLF, and each of those comparisons fails on content that
# is correct.
#
# The list was enumerated twice and both lists were incomplete, one release round trip each:
#
#   first  THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, SOURCE_OFFER.md, Cargo.lock
#          -> missed LICENSE, whose digest AND byte length are inside the notice
#             (LF 35149 bytes 3972dc97...; CRLF 35823 bytes 230184f6...; the 674-byte
#              difference is exactly GPL-3.0's line count)
#   second + LICENSE, COPYRIGHT, Cargo.toml
#          -> missed resources/icons/*.svg, DESIGN_SYSTEM_PIN.json and qml/RedrobTokens.qml,
#             which build_app_icon.py --check and generate_design_tokens.py --check compare,
#             so the design-system guard failed on a pristine tree
#
# A third enumeration would be the same mistake again. The rule is the whole tree, because the
# question is not "which files happen to be compared today".
#
# `-text` and NOT `* text=auto eol=lf`. The difference matters: text=auto normalises on STAGING,
# which would rewrite the 23 CRLF sequences THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md legitimately carries from
# vendored licence text -- silently changing the bytes of the artifact whose bytes are the
# contract. `-text` tells git to convert in NEITHER direction, so no committed blob moves;
# verified with `git add --renormalize .` reporting no change.
#
# The cost, stated rather than discovered later: a contributor whose editor writes CRLF will
# commit CRLF, since nothing normalises it on the way in. The distribution check reports that
# directly now ("carry more CRLF line endings than the generated content does"), which is the
# trade this repository wants -- a visible failure beats a silent rewrite of a hashed artifact.
* -text

# Binary assets, declared explicitly rather than left to auto-detection. `-text` already stops
# conversion, so this is about intent and about diff behaviour: a corrupted icon is not a build
# failure, it renders as a blank frame.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.abr binary
*.gbr binary
*.gih binary
*.icc binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
